# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Large generated deliverables (> ~5 MB)
#
# Rendered posters are regenerated from tracked sources, and every export would
# otherwise add a fresh multi-megabyte blob to history. They are ignored here;
# `python tools/make_snapshots.py` writes a small PNG preview of each into
# snapshots/, which IS tracked, so the repository still shows what was produced.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
output/pdf/
tmp/

# Generated posters (HTML ~10 MB, PDF ~7 MB each, both regenerable from the
# notebook). The templates are source and stay tracked.
poster/poster_*.html
poster/poster_*.pdf
!poster/poster_template.html
!poster/poster_template_90x210.html

# PNG previews of the above are deliberately committed.
!snapshots/
!snapshots/*.png

# Exception: source figures that notebooks read as inputs stay tracked even
# when they exceed the size threshold. spatial_tissue_validation_panel.png is
# 7.5 MB and is embedded by poster/generate_poster.ipynb; ignoring it, or
# down-sampling it to fit, would break reproduction from a fresh clone
# (palette quantisation to get under 5 MB introduced errors up to 61/255 on
# the expression colour map).
!sclc_validation/spatial_validation/figures/spatial_tissue_validation_panel.png

# OS and editor files
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
.idea/
.vscode/

# The poster and talk sources are kept out of the repository entirely, by
# request - neither directory appears on GitHub. Both stay in the working tree
# and are unaffected locally; this only controls what is published.
#
# Consequence worth knowing: the poster and the deck are no longer reproducible
# from a clone. generate_poster.ipynb, poster_template.html, build_deck.js and
# make_assets.py all live only on the machines that already have them, so they
# are not backed up by this repository either. The figure generators under
# tools/ and sclc_validation/*/scripts/ are NOT affected and stay tracked.
poster/
talk/

# Built poster and talk artifacts. Sources are tracked, outputs are not:
# everything below is reproducible from committed code, so it lives on disk
# rather than in history.
#
#   node talk/build_deck.js        -> JSDP_P25_talk.pptx   (then export the PDF)
#   python talk/make_assets.py     -> talk/assets/*.png
#   poster/generate_poster.ipynb   -> poster/poster_*.html and .pdf
#
# talk/assets/*.png were previously kept tracked on the grounds that they are
# inputs to build_deck.js rather than outputs, and losing them would leave a
# clone unable to build the deck at all. make_assets.py removed that argument:
# it rebuilds every asset from the poster's own figures, so they are now
# derived files like the rest.
talk/JSDP_P25_talk.pptx
talk/JSDP_P25_talk.pdf
talk/assets/*.png

# Node dependencies for the talk-deck generator (talk/build_deck.js).
node_modules/

# Superseded poster drafts. Only the final poster's PNG preview is tracked;
# every draft is regenerable from the notebook by setting DRAFT_LABEL.
snapshots/poster__poster_draft_*.png

# Snapshots: only the preview README.md puts on the repository's front page is
# tracked. The full-board export's preview is not shown anywhere, and previews
# of a git-ignored PDF are not worth carrying.
snapshots/output__pdf__*.png
poster/poster_draft_*
poster/posterdown_draft*

# R session leftovers
.Rhistory
